 "sources": [
  {
   "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lockheed_C-130A_Hercules_(late)_3-view_line_drawing.png",
   "title": "USAF, T.O. 1C-130A-2-2, page 1-3: the C-130A Hercules (late, radome nose) side, plan and front views with their dimensions (a scan on Wikimedia Commons, public domain as a USAF work)",
   "accessed": "2026-11-22",
   "note": "Outlines read off the three views (tools/trace/c30j/); the figures are the aircraft's measurements, not a copy of the drawing. A technical order's dimensioned drawing, not a manufacturer's airport-planning sheet (none is public for any C-130): a 1797 x 2566 px scan of about 39 px a meter. The C-130J keeps the C-130's fuselage (the same 97 ft 9 in), wing, tail and nacelle outlines, so the airframe is the C-130A's here. Scaled by its printed 97 ft 9 in (29.79 m) overall length; its 29 ft 4 in wheelbase is drawn within 0.1% at that scale, its plan's span and its front view's within 0.2% of the printed 132 ft 8 in (each view rendered to the side's scale: the plan is drawn 3.1% short lengthwise, evened out). It doesn't quite draw its own figures elsewhere: the side view puts the propellers' plane 0.21 m behind the printed 29 ft 11 in from the nose, which the plan draws (the nacelles and propellers are moved to it, the wing staying as drawn); the tailplane is 1.5% wider than its printed 52 ft 8 in (kept as drawn); the crown is drawn 4.40 m above the ground line where it prints 15 ft (4.57 m), and the fin's top 11.45 m where it prints 38 ft 4 in: the fuselage's drawn height (3.88 m; the front view 3.84 m) is kept, as a side-on photo of an HC-130J bears out (below). The fuselage is drawn 10-15 px (0.08-0.13 m) off the wing's middle in the plan and front views: each view's centerline is halfway between, so every part is within 0.07 m of either. The front view draws the tailplane as one line along the wing's upper surface: its height is the side view's (0.31 m under the crown at its root). Main gear sponsons are left out of the belly (bridged); the crew entrance door (left) and the paratroop door behind the sponson (both sides) as drawn; the C-130A's forward cargo door and round windows aren't the C-130J's and are left out (no cabin windows). The flight deck's glass is the C-130J's, from the HC-130J photos (Juneau, Barbers Point, Grand Forks) placed against the drawing's panes, which the late C-130A shares within about 0.1 m: the front pane each side carried forward to its center post on the crown from the front view (0.86-1.32 m up at the centerline, where the front view draws the center panes' bottom and top), three side panes under three eyebrow panes (the C-130A's fourth, forward eyebrow isn't the J's), one chin window under the front pane, and the tall side window by the number (the C-130A's two small ones there aren't the J's); the photo, taken a little from ahead, draws the side's panes up to 0.1 m aft of the drawing's. Confidence medium."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.easa.europa.eu/en/downloads/7845/en",
   "title": "EASA, Type-Certificate Data Sheet P.087, R391 series propellers (Dowty Propellers, Issue 03, 11 December 2018)",
   "accessed": "2026-11-22",
   "note": "The C-130J's propeller, R391/6-132-F/3: six composite blades, 411.5 cm across (the shape's 4.115 m; the drawing's discs are the C-130A's 15 ft 1 in)."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.lockheedmartin.com/content/dam/lockheed-martin/aero/documents/C-130J/C130JPocketGuide.pdf",
   "title": "Lockheed Martin, C-130J Super Hercules Pocket Guide: General Arrangement and General Characteristics (pp. 6-7)",
   "accessed": "2026-11-22",
   "note": "The C-130J-30's general arrangement (span 132 ft 7 in, height 38 ft 10 in, fuselage 14 ft 2 in wide; four AE2100D3s with Dowty R391 six-blade propellers). A brochure illustration not drawn to one scale (its side view 10% longer than its plan against its own figures), so a check only: the J's nacelles are drawn in the same places and outline as the C-130A's; its 14 ft 2 in is the shape's fuselage width check (+1.6% by line centres)."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juneau_USCG_C130_55.jpg",
   "title": "USCG HC-130J 2002 at Juneau, 2019-08-19, right side (CC BY-SA 4.0)",
   "accessed": "2026-11-22",
   "note": "A check only (only measurements are taken): flipped nose-left and fitted at the nose, tail cone, fin tip and the fuselage's height 5.9 m back (tools/photo_trace.py), the outline lands on the crown, belly, nose and the fin's root and trailing edge within about 0.1 m; the fin's leading edge near its top is up to 0.3 m forward of the shape's in the photo (taken a little from ahead)."
  }
